<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tierra de Campos</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Tierra de Campos is a flat, largely treeless region of the northern Meseta in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a> and León, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, spreading across the provinces of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palencia">Palencia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valladolid">Valladolid</a>, León and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zamora">Zamora</a>. Resettled by Christian farmers in the 10th century as the frontier moved south, it became one of Spain's main cereal-growing areas and is known for its adobe villages and dovecotes.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tierra-de-campos.jpg" alt="Tierra de Campos" width="320" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Andrecp · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Moforlogiaagr%C3%A1ria.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
